{"purpose":"Determine which of walkie's stated requirements are already satisfied by the Tailscale substrate, so that the build does not spend effort duplicating solved problems — and to identify precisely where the substrate's protection ends.","content":"## Evidence basis and its limits\n\nThis finding is drawn from established platform documentation and from the analysis recorded in this planning session. It has **not** been empirically measured on the team's actual tailnet.\n\nTwo claims in particular are calculated rather than observed and must be validated before anything depends on them:\n\n- the round-trip cost added by a DERP relay hop on this team's topology;\n- whether direct paths actually form between the specific device pairs in use, or whether the tailnet is quietly relaying most traffic.\n\nThe second is the more dangerous unknown: a hybrid design that falls back to relay will silently mask a tailnet that never achieves direct connectivity. This is why the observability baseline treats the direct-versus-relay ratio as a first-class metric rather than a diagnostic afterthought.\n\n## What this changes downstream\n\n- Authentication is a solved problem — a WhoIs lookup at connection accept time.\n- Transport encryption for direct paths is a solved problem.\n- Application-level encryption is scoped narrowly, to the coordinator's blind spots, rather than applied uniformly.\n- The coordinator's role shrinks from 'relay everything' to 'coordinate, and relay only when a direct path fails'.","investigationSummary":"Examined the capabilities the tailnet already exposes to an application running on it: the WireGuard data plane between nodes; automatic NAT traversal with DERP relay fallback; the stable per-device CGNAT addressing range 100.64.0.0/10; node identity and tailnet ACLs; the local API's WhoIs lookup, which maps a connection's remote address to an authenticated tailnet identity; and tsnet, which lets a Go program join the tailnet as its own node rather than depending on a host daemon.","conclusion":"**Four capabilities are already provided and must not be rebuilt.** (1) Confidentiality and integrity of traffic in transit, node to node. (2) NAT traversal, including automatic fallback to a DERP relay when a direct path cannot be established. (3) Stable addressing per device. (4) Authenticated peer identity, retrievable server-side from the connection's remote address via WhoIs.\n\nThe consequences are large and reduce scope directly: walkie needs **no STUN/TURN implementation**, and **no password, token, session store or user database** — authentication is a lookup, and authorization is a tailnet ACL.\n\n**Two capabilities are not provided, and these are walkie's actual problem space.** (1) Application-level presence with liveness semantics — the tailnet knows a node exists, not whether a person is at it. (2) Store-and-forward delivery for devices that are offline when a message is sent.\n\n**One residual risk.** Traffic that passes through walkie's own coordinator leaves the WireGuard tunnel's protection at that coordinator: messages parked on disk in the offline queue, and payloads taking the relay-fallback path. Those two cases — and only those two — are in scope for application-level encryption."}